{"name":"Apple Cider","hex":"#da995f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"apple cider","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Cider ; fermented apple juice.","Cider ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/apple_cider"}],"entries":[{"title":"Apple cider","blocks":[{"text":"Apple cider is the name used in the United States and Canada for an unfiltered, unsweetened, non-alcoholic beverage made from apples. Apple cider should not be confused with the alcoholic beverage known as cider in the rest of the world, which is called \"hard cider\" in the US. Outside of the United States and Canada, it is commonly referred to as cloudy apple juice to distinguish it from clearer, filtered apple juice and hard cider."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Cider_and_apple_juice.jpg/330px-Cider_and_apple_juice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_cider","wikidata":"Q5977438"},{"title":"Apple","blocks":[{"text":"An apple is the round, edible fruit of an apple tree. Fruit trees of the orchard or domestic apple, the most widely grown in the genus, are cultivated worldwide. The tree originated in Central Asia, where its wild ancestor, Malus sieversii, is still found. Apples have been grown for thousands of years in Eurasia before they were introduced to the American continent by European colonists. Apples have cultural significance in many mythologies and religions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg/330px-Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple","wikidata":"Q89"},{"title":"Cider","blocks":[{"text":"Cider is an alcoholic beverage made from the fermented juice of apples. The juice of most varieties of apple can be used to make cider, but cider apples are most desirable. The addition of sugar or extra fruit before a second fermentation increases the ethanol content of the resulting beverage."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Midwest_hard_cider.jpg/330px-Midwest_hard_cider.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cider","wikidata":"Q167296"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Juicing3.jpg/500px-Juicing3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Juicing3.jpg/500px-Juicing3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A vintage combination apple grinder and press. Moving slatted baskets left to right allows simultaneous two-person production.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJuicing3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Juicing1.jpg/500px-Juicing1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Juicing1.jpg/500px-Juicing1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A small scale hydraulic apple press. Each load produces about 140 US gallons (530 L)/(116 Imperial gallons).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJuicing1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Juicing2.jpg/500px-Juicing2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Juicing2.jpg/500px-Juicing2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cidering in a contemporary rural area mill. Custom batches pressed directly to bulk containers on demand.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJuicing2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipe